course: dictionary
lesson: 15615
title: Tasteless: three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Tasteless is a word you can work out from its parts. Taste means flavour, and -less means without, so tasteless means without much flavour.
explain: «The soup was tasteless, so Mum added a pinch of salt.» The salt is the clue. People add salt when food needs more flavour, so tasteless means it has little or no flavour.
explain: Tasteless also has two other meanings. «His joke was tasteless and made everyone go quiet.» Here it means rude or unkind. Ask yourself what is being described, then pick the meaning that fits.
fact: Tasteless means having little or no flavour, like plain water.
fact: Tasteless can also mean rude, unkind or not suitable, as in «a tasteless joke».
fact: Tasteless can describe clothes or decorations that look cheap or ugly.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «The soup was tasteless, so Mum added a pinch of salt.» What does tasteless mean here? | Very spicy and hot | Cold to touch | *Having little or no flavour | Full of bright colours
exercise: «The comedian's tasteless joke upset the whole audience.» What does tasteless suggest? | Long and boring | Quiet and gentle | *Rude and unkind | Funny and clever
exercise: Which of these is an example of a tasteless decoration? | A bowl of warm soup | *A cheap, ugly gold ornament covering a whole wall | A small wooden chair | A glass of plain water
test: Science lessons say water is tasteless, odourless and colourless. What does tasteless mean here? | Painted in bright colours | *Has no taste at all | Smells very strong | Hard and cold
test: Which sentence uses tasteless to mean rude or unkind? | «The lemonade was tasteless and cold.» | «The cake was tasteless and dry.» | *«Her tasteless remark made her friend cry.»
exam: Jayden says tasteless always means the food has no flavour. Is he right? | No, it only means very sweet | *No, it can also mean rude, unkind or cheap-looking, as in a tasteless joke | Yes, because the word has the word taste in it | Yes, it only ever describes food
